# AmpHive TLS reverse proxy (Caddy) — REFERENCE ONLY.
#
# You normally never copy this file anywhere. deploy/scripts/deploy.ps1 does
# NOT generate or touch the Caddyfile — that was true of an earlier two-VM-era
# architecture, but the 2026-08-02 relay rewrite deliberately dropped it (see
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md's "Deploy script" section: the Caddyfile is one of the
# things deploy.ps1 explicitly does NOT ship). The only generator today is
# deploy/relay/deploy-relay.sh, which writes a starter Caddyfile from
# CADDY_DOMAIN / CADDY_CPO_DOMAIN / ACME_EMAIL in .env on a fresh relay's
# FIRST deploy only; after that the live Caddyfile is operator-managed by hand
# on the VM (deploy-relay.sh will not overwrite an existing one). This example
# mirrors what that generator produces — if you change a header here, change
# it in deploy-relay.sh too (and in frontend/nginx.conf, which carries the
# same set for the non-Caddy paths). The only intentional structural
# difference is the (amphive_headers) snippet below: it is a readability
# device for this reference file, while deploy-relay.sh emits the same header
# block longhand per site (it's a shell heredoc and has no snippets). The
# HEADERS THEMSELVES must stay byte-identical across both files.
#
# Caddy automatically obtains and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate for the
# domain (HTTP-01 challenge on :80), persisted in the caddy_data volume.
#
# Requirements:
#   - CADDY_DOMAIN (and CADDY_CPO_DOMAIN, if set) resolve to the VM's public IP.
#   - GCP firewall allows inbound tcp:80 AND tcp:443 to the VM. Port 80 must
#     stay open even though the named sites only redirect on it: that is where
#     the ACME HTTP-01 challenge is answered, and Caddy serves
#     /.well-known/acme-challenge/* ahead of any site route, so the redirects
#     below do not break certificate renewal.

# Global options. trusted_proxies (M3; updated 2026-09-28 for the Cloudflare
# front door): amphive.app and cpo.amphive.app now sit behind Cloudflare's
# free HTTP proxy (mqtt.amphive.app and mail.amphive.app stay DNS-only / grey
# cloud — MQTT 8883 and SMTP/IMAP can't traverse the CF HTTP proxy), so
# Caddy's TCP peer is a Cloudflare edge IP, not the visitor, and the private
# compose-network ranges this block used to list no longer apply. The ranges
# below are Cloudflare's published edge IPs (refresh occasionally from
# https://www.cloudflare.com/ips-v4 and /ips-v6 — they change rarely).
# Combined with `client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP`, Caddy trusts the
# CF-Connecting-IP header ONLY when the request arrives from one of these
# ranges, and writes the upstream X-Forwarded-For as that single vouched
# client IP — any XFF the visitor tried to spoof is discarded. From an
# untrusted peer (e.g. someone hitting the origin IP directly, bypassing
# Cloudflare — deliberately still possible; the GCP firewall was NOT locked to
# these ranges because mail.amphive.app's Let's Encrypt HTTP-01 renewal needs
# :80 reachable directly) CF-Connecting-IP is ignored and XFF falls back to
# the real peer IP, so a direct hit can't spoof identity either. Either way
# the backend's per-IP rate limiter (backend rate_limit.py) sees a clean
# single-value XFF it can trust. Verified with `caddy validate` + reload
# against the live Caddyfile, and the semantics tested on caddy v2.11.4 in a
# throwaway container.
{
	# email you@example.com   # uncomment for Let's Encrypt expiry notices
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static 173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 103.22.200.0/22 103.31.4.0/22 141.101.64.0/18 108.162.192.0/18 190.93.240.0/20 188.114.96.0/20 197.234.240.0/22 198.41.128.0/17 162.158.0.0/15 104.16.0.0/13 104.24.0.0/14 172.64.0.0/13 131.0.72.0/22 2400:cb00::/32 2606:4700::/32 2803:f800::/32 2405:b500::/32 2405:8100::/32 2a06:98c0::/29 2c0f:f248::/32
		client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP
	}
}

# Snippet so the two named sites and the bare-IP fallback cannot drift apart.
# Caddy `header <field> <value>` SETS (replaces) the field, so these are the one
# authoritative copy even though the frontend nginx also emits an identical set.
# Keep byte-identical with frontend/nginx.conf and deploy/relay/deploy-relay.sh.
#
#   CSP            mirrors nginx: razorpay script/frame-src, 'unsafe-inline'
#                  styles, self-hosted data: fonts, wss: websockets, plus
#                  `object-src 'none'` and `frame-ancestors 'none'`
#                  (frame-ancestors is the modern replacement for
#                  X-Frame-Options; X-Frame-Options stays as the legacy
#                  companion for older embedded WebViews).
#   Permissions-   geolocation is `self`, NOT denied: /map genuinely calls
#   Policy         navigator.geolocation to centre the map on the driver, and a
#                  blanket geolocation=() silently breaks it. Everything else
#                  the app never uses is denied. If Razorpay Checkout is ever
#                  enabled and needs the W3C Payment Request API, relax
#                  payment=() to payment=(self "https://api.razorpay.com")
#                  rather than dropping the directive.
(amphive_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(self), camera=(), microphone=(), usb=(), payment=(), interest-cohort=()"
		Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://*.razorpay.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https: wss:; frame-src https://*.razorpay.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
	}
}

amphive.app {
	encode gzip
	import amphive_headers

	# WebSocket + API + SPA all live behind the frontend nginx container, which
	# already proxies /api and /socket.io to the backend. Caddy just terminates
	# TLS and forwards everything to it. header_up pins the upstream's
	# X-Forwarded-For to the single {client_ip} the trusted_proxies block above
	# resolved (Cloudflare's vouched CF-Connecting-IP when behind Cloudflare,
	# the real peer otherwise) instead of Caddy's default of appending to
	# whatever XFF the client sent — so frontend-nginx's own append still lands
	# the backend's TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=2 exactly where rate_limit.py expects it.
	reverse_proxy frontend:80 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
	}
}

cpo.amphive.app {
	encode gzip
	import amphive_headers
	reverse_proxy frontend:80 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
	}
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plain HTTP on the NAMED domains: redirect, never serve.
#
# This used to be covered by the catch-all `http://` block below, which SERVED
# the app over cleartext for every Host including the real domains. The comment
# justifying that called it a DuckDNS-outage escape hatch and said to "tighten
# to a redir once a paid/reliable domain is in place" — amphive.app went live
# 2026-07-20, so that justification has expired. Leaving it as-is means a
# disaster-recovery rebuild silently reopens a window where
# http://amphive.app/api/... answers 200 with a bearer JWT in the Authorization
# header, in the clear, and neither the browser nor the user is told.
#
# 308 (not 301) so a mis-addressed POST keeps its method and body instead of
# being silently downgraded to a GET — this is also what Caddy's own automatic
# HTTP→HTTPS redirect uses.
#
# Being explicit here also removes a genuine ambiguity in the old file. Caddy
# adds an automatic HTTP→HTTPS redirect for a site served over HTTPS *unless* a
# site block already claims that host on the HTTP port — and whether an
# empty-host `http://` catch-all counted as claiming `amphive.app` was never
# something the config stated, only something you found out by curling it. An
# explicit `http://amphive.app` block settles it: Caddy routes by matcher
# specificity, a site block with a hostname always beats the empty-host
# catch-all, and the automatic redirect is skipped in favour of this one. The
# ordering in the file is for human readers, not for Caddy.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
http://amphive.app {
	redir https://{host}{uri} 308
}

http://cpo.amphive.app {
	redir https://{host}{uri} 308
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bare-IP / unknown-Host requests over plain HTTP: still SERVED, deliberately.
#
# This is the one block that is NOT being converted to a redirect, and the
# reasoning is worth writing down because "redirect everything to https" reads
# like the obviously-correct answer:
#
#   1. There is no certificate for an IP address. Let's Encrypt does not issue
#      for bare IPs, so `redir https://{host}{uri}` from http://<ip>/ lands on
#      a TLS handshake Caddy cannot complete with a valid cert — a hard browser
#      interstitial. That is strictly worse than cleartext, because it is not
#      reachable at all.
#   2. Redirecting to the named domain instead defeats the entire purpose. The
#      escape hatch exists for the case where DNS is broken (seen with DuckDNS
#      2026-07-11); sending the browser back to a name that does not resolve is
#      a no-op with extra steps.
#   3. The frontend depends on this mode. src/utils/appHost.js treats a bare-IP
#      host as "unsplit" and App.jsx serves the COMBINED driver + operator +
#      admin route tree there specifically so a DNS outage does not lock
#      operators out of the console. Delete this site and that fallback has
#      nowhere to run.
#
# Accepted residual risk, stated plainly: traffic to this site — including /api
# and /socket.io with a bearer JWT — is cleartext. It is mitigated only by
# obscurity (you have to type the IP; nothing links to it, robots.txt and the
# canonical tags all name amphive.app) and by being the fallback rather than
# the path anyone normally takes. HSTS learned from https://amphive.app does
# NOT cover it: HSTS is host-scoped and, per spec, is never applied to IP
# literals. If the DNS-outage risk is ever retired, delete this block — do not
# convert it to a redirect.
#
# Same security headers as the named sites; HSTS is a harmless no-op over plain
# http — browsers ignore it per spec.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
http:// {
	encode gzip
	import amphive_headers
	reverse_proxy frontend:80 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
	}
}
